{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_011Cg2purgjUUoGmHfVvTsR6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a clear picture of what needs to be fixed. Let me analyze each issue:\n\n1. **graph/graph.go - `Order()` function**:\n   - Currently just returns names in non-deterministic map iteration order\n   - Needs: deterministic topological sort, cycle detection, self-cycle detection, missing dep detection\n\n2. **parse/parse.go - `Load()` function**:\n   - Currently just parses JSON, no validation\n   - Needs: reject empty names, reject duplicates, reject missing dependencies, reject cycles (though cycles could be detected in graph)\n\n3. **plan/plan.go - `Build()` function**:\n   - This just wires parse.Load and g.Order together, so it should be fine once those two are corrected.\n\n4. **cache/cache.go**: the cache currently only stores the last result without ever invalidating, so I need it to key off a hash of the input, properly invalidate on changed input, and never cache (or mask) an error result—errors should pass through cleanly and not poison the cache.\n\n5. **batch/batch.go**: the `Build()` function currently keeps going after an error and returns partial results; it needs to fail atomically, returning nil output and the first error encountered.\n\n6. **cmd/planner/main.go**: it currently swallows the stdin read error, so it needs to propagate failures with a nonzero exit code and newline-terminated output on success.\n\nNow I'm going through the test files in detail. The graph test checks deterministic ordering across repeated calls and verifies that a self-referencing cycle produces an error. The plan test checks that dependencies resolve into the right build order and that invalid cases (missing dep, cycle, duplicate, empty name) are rejected. The cache test walks through valid-invalid-valid inputs to confirm errors aren't cached and recovery works. The batch test uses a single shared cache across multiple builds, which makes me reconsider how caching interacts with repeated inputs in a batch.\n\nWith proper per-input cache invalidation, repeated inputs like a-b-a should each resolve correctly, matching the expected output sequence, and if any input in a batch fails, the whole batch should fail atomically with a nil result and an error. The CLI test expects plain comma-separated output with a trailing newline for valid builds, and a nonzero exit code when a cycle is detected. With all the test expectations mapped out, I'm ready to move on to implementing the actual fixes.\n\nI'm writing the graph package now: a `Graph` type backed by a map of node names to their dependency lists, with an `Order` method that first validates all referenced dependencies actually exist in the graph, returning an error if not. Then I'm implementing Kahn's algorithm for topological sorting, building an in-degree map to track dependency counts deterministically.\n\nImplementing Kahn's algorithm... I'm building a reverse dependency map and in-degree counts, then initializing the queue with zero in-degree nodes sorted alphabetically for deterministic output, and processing the queue to build the result.\n\nI also need to check my output against the expected deterministic order the test expects.\n\nVerifying with a/b case (both zero in-degree, alphabetical result), the lib/app/base chain (processed in dependency order), and a self-cycle, which correctly triggers cycle detection since in-degree never drops to zero. Missing dependencies should be caught by validation before the topological sort even runs.\n\nNow I'm writing the parse package, which decodes the JSON node list, checks for empty names or duplicate node entries, and builds the graph map. Missing deps and cycles stay out of this layer since graph.Order() already handles that validation.\n\nChecking the plan_test cases confirms the split: missing dep and cycle errors come from graph.Order, while duplicate and empty-name errors come from parse.Load, and both get exercised through plan.Build. Next I need to design the cache so it's keyed by the content of the input.\n\nI'm thinking the simplest implementation just tracks the last input and its result, invalidating the cache when the input changes, and critically never caching an error result -- the test expects that a broken build always surfaces its error rather than returning a stale cached success.\n\nTracing through the remaining cases confirms the cache works correctly for different keys, errors not being cached, and reverting to a previously seen key.\n\nNow I'm writing a batch package that uses the cache to build multiple inputs, returning early on the first error encountered.\n\nChecking this against the test case with inputs \"a\", \"b\", \"a\" in sequence—since each has a distinct key, the cache will correctly produce \"a\", then \"b\", then \"a\" again on the repeat.\n\nNow I'm setting up main.go for the planner command, reading input from stdin and preparing error handling before passing the data along.\n\nI'm also reconsidering the determinism of the topological sort — sorting the queue each iteration works but is inefficient; a cleaner approach would maintain insertion order while still guaranteeing consistent output for ties.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1455,"cache_read_input_tokens":17260,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1455,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":12,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"52f7d276-1f81-442d-a322-87c6d05bcfde","uuid":"0611e78a-cdf3-4869-953a-f820766aa288"}
